          <p>{item.stage2}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    This is a genuinely different caution from ordinary "hiring is selective" competitiveness that
    shows up in policy consulting, CSR, or economics research roles. In those tracks, one strong
    portfolio can open the door, and your growth from there depends mostly on the work you keep
    producing. In academia and government welfare recruitment, a second, separate filter — a scarce
    permanent-post market on one side, seniority-based promotion on the other — sits between "you
    made it" and "you actually reached the pay and scope people picture when they imagine the role."
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">What AI is doing to social science careers</h2>

  <p>
    Every track on this list touches research, writing, and analysis — exactly the layer AI tools
    are changing fastest. The honest picture has a real risk side and a real opportunity side, and
    they are not the same task.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="comparison-col comparison-col--risk">
      <div class="comparison-col__label">Where AI is already automating the work</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Routine survey coding and open-ended response tagging — sorting hundreds of free-text answers into themes — is already shifting into AI-assisted workflows at research agencies and CSR teams.</li>
        <li>First-pass literature review summaries and basic transcription of interviews or focus groups are easier to automate than the interpretation that follows them.</li>
        <li>Basic economic data cleaning and first-draft chart generation — turning a raw government dataset into a readable table — is now largely a tool problem AI handles faster than a fresher can by hand.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="comparison-col comparison-col--grow">
      <div class="comparison-col__label">Where AI is genuinely creating leverage</div>
      <ul>
        <li>AI-assisted large-scale qualitative coding: feeding hundreds of interview transcripts into an AI tool for a fast first-pass theme map, then spending your real time on the interpretation — what the pattern actually means for a specific community, client, or policy decision.</li>
        <li>Data-driven CSR impact measurement and economic forecasting: combining programme data with AI-assisted modelling to show a funder or a bank exactly what changed, which is exactly why data-literate sociologists and economists are being pulled into higher-paying consulting and BFSI roles.</li>
        <li>Independent research or consulting platforms at scale: one person with real fieldwork credibility and AI-assisted analysis and drafting can now credibly run a research or evaluation practice that used to need a small team — a direct multiplier on Track 1's ownership advantage.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    <strong>Right now:</strong> learn to use AI tools to speed up transcription, coding, and
    first-pass data summaries, but keep manually verifying every finding and citation before it goes
    into a report under your name — a wrong statistic in a published evaluation or brief is a
    credibility problem, not just an embarrassment.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>As adoption matures:</strong> the differentiated value shifts further toward fieldwork
    judgment, community trust, and original data collection — running your own survey, doing your
    own interviews — that a general-purpose AI tool cannot originate on its own. That is a strong
    argument for building data literacy alongside your fieldwork or writing skill now, rather than
    treating "I understand people" as a complete skill set on its own.
  </p>

