  <h2 id="skills">Skills that matter on every track</h2>

  <p>
    A social science degree gives you a starting point — a way of reading how people, institutions,
    and markets behave. What turns that starting point into a real, high-income skill portfolio, and
    eventually earlier financial freedom, is a deliberate stack built on top of it, not the degree
    alone.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Research method and data literacy</strong> — comfort with survey design, interview technique, and basic statistical or spreadsheet analysis. This is the multiplier skill that separates a median researcher or social worker from one who gets hired into consulting, CSR, or BFSI roles.</li>
    <li><strong>Clear, plain-language writing</strong> — a policy brief, an impact report, or a case note is worthless if only other specialists can follow it. This is the proof format most of these tracks actually hire on.</li>
    <li><strong>Field and relationship judgment</strong> — the human layer behind social work, CSR execution, and consulting that AI does not touch. Trust and relationship management with communities and clients stay entirely human.</li>
    <li><strong>Programme and monitoring-and-evaluation thinking</strong> — the ability to define what "worked" actually means and measure it, which is exactly what turns fieldwork experience into CSR or consulting-level pay.</li>
    <li><strong>Practical AI fluency</strong> — using AI tools to speed up coding, transcription, and drafting while personally verifying every output, per the AI section above.</li>
  </ul>

