  <h2 id="college">The college and degree decision — an honest take</h2>

  <p>
    Social science is another field where the pattern most students assume — "the best option is
    the most expensive one" — often runs backwards. The Delhi School of Economics, Jawaharlal Nehru
    University's sociology and social work departments, and the Tata Institute of Social Sciences
    (TISS) for the MSW route are simultaneously among the strongest departments in the country for
    their subject and considerably cheaper than most private alternatives, because they are public
    or heavily subsidised institutions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Newer private liberal-arts universities — Ashoka, O.P. Jindal Global University, Symbiosis,
    FLAME — pitch smaller class sizes, global exposure, and stronger placement support, at a
    genuinely different price, often ₹8-15 lakh or more for the full undergraduate or postgraduate
    programme.
  </p>

  <p>
    Test any spending above the conservative starting benchmark of roughly 10% of your total
    education budget against real, course-specific evidence: does the private option's faculty,
    fieldwork placement network, and peer group create an advantage you genuinely cannot build
    yourself through a public-college department plus deliberate outside work — an NGO internship,
    a published research note, or a CSR-implementation-partner placement? If you cannot point to a
    specific advantage, the extra spending is a prestige purchase, not a career investment.
  </p>

  <p>
    Wherever you study, the material that actually builds real analytical skill is inexpensive or
    free: government survey data from the Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation
    (MoSPI), Economic and Political Weekly, published CSR and NGO impact reports from established
    foundations, and public-policy course material many universities now publish openly. A student
    who reads this consistently for a year builds a stronger, more current foundation than one who
    paid more for a degree and read none of it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="proof">Building proof before you commit years</h2>

  <p>
    A qualification checklist tells you what is formally required. It cannot tell you whether you
    will actually be good at the work, or enjoy it, before you spend years finding out the hard way.
    Test that first, using <strong>The 3 Gates</strong>.
  </p>

  <div class="gates-block">
    <p class="gates-block__title">
      Do not start NET-JRF prep, enrol in a PhD, or take a CDPO-exam coaching package before passing
      all three gates below.
    </p>
